</div></div></div><div class="fe-block fe-block-yui_3_17_2_1_1785868308488_767"><div class="sqs-block website-component-block sqs-block-website-component sqs-block-html html-block" data-block-css="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/98408bca-32b6-4c92-a1e4-edf736d87627_1075/website.components.html.styles.css&quot;]" data-block-scripts="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/98408bca-32b6-4c92-a1e4-edf736d87627_1075/website.components.html.visitor.js&quot;]" data-block-type="1337" data-definition-name="website.components.html" data-sqsp-block="text" data-website-component-id="yui_3_17_2_1_1785868308488_767" id="block-yui_3_17_2_1_1785868308488_767"><div class="sqs-block-content"><div class="sqs-text-block-container">
  
  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Power outages can happen at any time, especially during severe summer storms, winter weather, and other emergencies. When the power goes out, keeping food at safe temperatures becomes a challenge. Knowing what food is safe to keep and what should be thrown away can help prevent foodborne illness.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">The Michigan Poison &amp; Drug Information Center (MiPDC) recommends following these steps to protect yourself and your family during a power outage.</p><h2 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>What to Do When the Power Goes Out</strong></span></h2><h3 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>Keep Refrigerator and Freezer Doors Closed</strong></span></h3><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">A refrigerator can keep food safely cold for about <strong>4 hours</strong> if the door remains closed.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">A full freezer can maintain a safe temperature for about <strong>48 hours</strong>, while a half-full freezer may keep food frozen for about <strong>24 hours</strong>.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Every time you open the refrigerator or freezer door, cold air escapes and food warms more quickly. Keep doors closed as much as possible until power is restored.</p><h2 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>How to Know If Food Is Safe</strong></span></h2><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Food that has been kept at unsafe temperatures for too long can allow harmful bacteria to grow. You cannot always tell if food is contaminated by looking at it, smelling it, or tasting it.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>When in doubt, throw it out.</strong></p><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Do not taste food to determine whether it is safe.</p></div>


  
