  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><h2 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>What About Food in the Freezer?</strong></span></h2><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Frozen foods that still contain ice crystals or have remained at <strong>40°F or below</strong> can usually be refrozen or cooked safely.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">However, discard frozen food if:</p><ul data-rte-list="default"><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">It has completely thawed and has been above 40°F for more than 2 hours</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">It has an unusual smell or appearance</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">It has been contaminated by floodwater</p></li></ul><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">A freezer thermometer can help you know whether frozen foods have stayed at a safe temperature.</p></div>


  
