  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><h2 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>Tips for Preparing for a Power Outage</strong></span></h2><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Planning ahead can help protect your food during an emergency.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Before severe weather:</p><ul data-rte-list="default"><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Keep appliance thermometers in your refrigerator and freezer</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Store a supply of shelf-stable foods</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Freeze containers of water to help keep food cold</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Know where to find coolers and ice</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Avoid opening refrigerator and freezer doors unnecessarily</p></li></ul><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">During an extended outage, consider using coolers with ice or frozen gel packs to keep perishable foods at safe temperatures.</p></div>


  
