  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><h2 style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><span class="sqsrte-text-color--darkAccent"><strong>What If I Ate Food That Was Not Safe?</strong></span></h2><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Eating food that has spoiled or been contaminated can cause foodborne illness, also called food poisoning.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Symptoms may include:</p><ul data-rte-list="default"><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Nausea</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Vomiting</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Diarrhea</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Stomach cramps</p></li><li><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Fever</p></li></ul><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">If you believe you or someone in your family became sick after eating questionable food, the Michigan Poison &amp; Drug Information Center can help determine what steps to take.</p><p class="isSelectedEnd" style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Call the Poison Help line anytime: <strong>1-800-222-1222</strong></p><p style="white-space:pre-wrap;" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Our poison specialists are available 24/7 to provide guidance for possible food exposures and answer questions about symptoms, risks, and next steps.</p></div>


  
